
  Bring each worktree's branch up to its branch on origin, never merging: origin is fetched once,
  then each worktree behind it moves in turn. A failure does not stop the others.
  Without arguments, the wizard asks which worktrees to fast-forward, those behind origin
  checked; unattended, those behind origin are fast-forwarded. A worktree is named by its
  branch or its path; --all asks for every worktree.

  A worktree ahead of origin, diverged from it, not on origin, in the middle of a rebase, a
  bisect or a merge, or whose directory is gone is left as it is, with what moves it on.

  A worktree behind origin with uncommitted changes is unsafe: named, it refuses the whole run
  before anything moves, unless --force; not named, it is kept as it is, with the line that
  forces it. --force lifts that refusal only: it still asks to confirm, unless --yes.

  Usage:
    wtm-next fast-forward [worktree...] [flags]

  Aliases:
    fast-forward, ff

  Examples:
    # Pick the worktrees to fast-forward
    wtm fast-forward

    wtm fast-forward feat/login

    # Every worktree, no prompts
    wtm ff --all --yes

    # See the plan first, then apply exactly that plan
    wtm fast-forward feat/login --dry-run
    wtm fast-forward feat/login --expect-fingerprint <hash>

    # For a script or an agent, with the v2 JSON envelope
    wtm fast-forward --all --yes --output json

  Flags:
        --all                         Fast-forward every worktree; one with uncommitted changes is left as it is unless --force
        --ask                         Ask again the questions this repository remembers an answer to, to change or forget it
    -n, --dry-run                     Show the plan and its fingerprint, apply nothing
        --expect-fingerprint string   Apply only if the facts still have this fingerprint, the one --dry-run printed
        --force                       Fast-forward a worktree that has uncommitted changes
    -h, --help                        help for fast-forward
        --output string               Output format: text or json (default "text")
    -y, --yes                         Run unattended: no question, no confirmation (without worktrees, those behind origin; a worktree named with uncommitted changes still refuses the run unless --force)

  Global Flags:
    -q, --quiet   Silence human output; errors and the exit code are unaffected, and --output json still emits its document

